WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.812
Welcome to Module 4 Aggregating Data with PostgreSQL
In the previous module

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.750
we focused mainly on individual rows how to retrieve
them filter them

00:00:09.938 --> 00:00:14.825
and change them safely In this module we move into
analytical SQL

00:00:15.688 --> 00:00:18.900
Instead of asking only which rows do I need we will
often

00:00:18.925 --> 00:00:22.550
ask what do these rows mean as a group This is where

00:00:22.562 --> 00:00:26.962
aggregate functions become useful COUNT can tell us
how many rows or

00:00:26.988 --> 00:00:32.212
values we have SUM can give us totals AVG can give
us

00:00:32.237 --> 00:00:35.750
averages while MIN and MAX help us find the smallest
and largest

00:00:35.762 --> 00:00:39.788
values We will combine those functions with GROUP BY
so that one

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:44.675
large table can become a meaningful summary report
The most important idea

00:00:44.688 --> 00:00:49.412
in this module is grain Grain means what one output
row represents

00:00:50.275 --> 00:00:53.662
Before writing GROUP BY try to say the result in
plain language

00:00:54.538 --> 00:00:58.688
For example one row per diagnosis one row per sales
category or

00:00:58.712 --> 00:01:03.050
one row per department Once that is clear the SQL
becomes much

00:01:03.075 --> 00:01:07.500
easier to design We will also compare WHERE and
HAVING build conditional

00:01:07.525 --> 00:01:11.438
metrics with CASE and FILTER create subtotal reports
with ROLLUP CUBE and

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:15.600
GROUPING SETS and finish with window functions that
add analytical context without

00:01:15.625 --> 00:01:19.925
collapsing the detail rows As you work keep one
habit in mind

00:01:20.138 --> 00:01:23.838
start with the question define the grain calculate
the measures apply the

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:27.250
right filters and then validate the result against
the detailed data
